<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Killala</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Killala is a village and small port on Killala Bay in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-mayo">County Mayo</a>, in the west of Ireland, and the seat of an ancient bishopric. On 22 August 1798 a French force under General Jean Humbert landed there to support the rebellion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/society-of-united-irishmen">United Irishmen</a>; after early success at Castlebar it surrendered at Ballinamuck, and government troops retook the town.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/killala.jpg" alt="Killala" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Liz McCabe · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Killala_street_scene_-_geograph.org.uk_-_486739.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Killala, in Irish Cill Ala, the mottled church, lies north of Ballina, with a harbour at the south end of Killala Bay. The village is in a civil parish of the same name. West of it the townland of Townsplots West, known locally as Enagh Beg, holds a number of ancient forts. Along the river nearby stand the ruins of several monasteries: Rosserk, a Franciscan house of strict observance founded in 1460, the Abbey of Moyne, and the Dominican Rathfran Friary north of the village.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Tradition links the church of Killala with Saint Patrick, who is said to have placed his disciple St. Muredach over it, as early as 442 or 443, and to have baptised 12,000 converts in a single day at a well near the town. Little is known of Muredach's successors before the 12th century. The Roman Catholic Diocese of Killala is one of the five suffragan sees of the Province of Tuam; John MacHale, later Archbishop of Tuam, was its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> in 1834. By the end of the 18th century Killala had a small sea port where fishing was the main work, and it produced coarse linens and woollens.</p><p>In August 1798 the force of General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-joseph-amable-humbert">Jean Joseph Amable Humbert</a>, 1,109 French troops sent to support the Irish Rebellion, seized Killala, took Ballina and on 27 August beat a larger government army under General Gerard Lake at the Battle of Castlebar. After the defeat at Ballinamuck, a government force under Major-General Eyre Power Trench attacked Killala on 23 September and drove out the last of the invaders. The town had been under rebel control for thirty-two days.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The village had 562 inhabitants at the 2016 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. Its 12th-century round tower, the last medieval structure of a monastic establishment, and the Cathedral Church of St. Patrick, built in the 1670s and one of two cathedral churches of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-ireland">Church of Ireland</a>'s Diocese of Tuam, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limerick">Limerick</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/killaloe">Killaloe</a>, still stand. A railway from Ballina reached Killala in 1893 and closed to goods in 1934. Since 1998, the bicentenary of the events of 1798, Killala has been twinned with Chauvé in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Killala” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The rebellion of 1798 broke out in May in Leinster, spread to Ulster and was crushed at Vinegar Hill and elsewhere; a French force under General Jean Humbert landed at <b>Killala</b> in August but surrendered.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ireland">Kingdom of Ireland · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
